  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 443 PM EDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nRidging remains the dominant synoptic weather feature at the start \nof the long term forecast period, with the latest guidance \ncollectively resolving a 595 decameter ridge directly above the \ncommonwealth from Monday to Wednesday. Midlevel heights of this \nmagnitude rank above the 99th percentile of model climatology in the \nblended LREF ensemble, and given that such values are the model \nconsensus, confidence is high that the Commonwealth of Kentucky will \nexperience hot and dry sensible weather conditions this week. The \nridging should eventually begin to break down by the end of the \nperiod, but this is marked with a potential transition to more \nactive northwesterly flow. Ridge-riding disturbances allow chances \nfor showers and storms to reenter the forecast for Thursday, Friday, \nand Saturday, but confidence in the forecast for wet weather at the \nend of the period is lower than it is for hot weather at the \nbeginning.\n\nEastern Kentucky is positioned directly to the east of the mid/upper \nlevel ridge axis. The related increase in subsidence aloft will \nfavor efficient diurnal processes for as long as the pattern \npersists. Expect mostly sunny skies and increasing temperatures \naccordingly, with ridge-valley temperature splits and river valley \nfog emerging at night. Modeled 850mb temperatures climb above the \n20 degree Celsius mark on Monday, remain elevated on Tuesday, and \nthen peak closer to 23 degrees Celsius on Wednesday. At the surface, \nthis should translate to widespread highs in the lower half of the \n90s on Monday and Tuesday before some locations flirt with the upper \n90s on Wednesday. The warmest temperatures will likely occur in the \nnorthern half of the forecast area (where heights and 850mb temps \nare the greatest) and in the deepest river valleys (where diurnal \nmixing will be deepest). Blended NBM temperatures are likely too \nwarm, and dewpoints likely too moist. As drier air mixes down from \nthe midlevels, dewpoints will likely remain below the 70 degree \nline. Heat indices may remain below the 105 degree heat headline \ncriteria mark, but it will still feel very hot outside. There won't \nbe much relief at night either, with ridge-valley splits above/below \nthe 70 degree mark. Interests with outdoor plans (such as high \nschool sports, recreational hikes, or camping) are encouraged to \nreview heat safety information at weather.gov/safety/heat in the \ncoming days. Take frequent breaks in the shade or indoors, stay \nhydrated, and continue to monitor for forecast updates. \n\nThe northeastern periphery of the ridge begins to flatten on \nThursday, which will allow a NW flow regime to set up over the Ohio \nRiver Valley. A series of shortwave impulses will then propagate \naround the ridge and towards the forecast area headed into next \nweekend, bringing about the next remarkable chances for showers and \nstorms. While 850mb temperatures will trend towards the 20C mark and \nmidlevel heights are progged to drop back down towards 590dm by \nthen, these disturbances will still be interacting with a seasonably \nwarm airmass. Models disagree on the amount of moisture available \nduring this pattern shift emerges, but if enough modified tropical \nmoisture makes it around the ridge and into the column, this late \nperiod activity will need to be monitored closely. The available \nmachine learning, analog-based, and AI-enhanced long-range \nconvective guidance is hinting at more active weather in the Greater \nAppalachians by the end of the period, but it is too early to \npinpoint specific timing and hazard details. With that being said, \nthe clouds and rain-cooled air associated with any convective \nactivity would likely mitigate the Major HeatRisk analyzed at the \nbeginning of the forecast period. Expect thermometer readings closer \nto climatological norms for the holiday weekend, but monitor for \nupdates to the convective forecast while planning any outdoor \nactivities for Friday and beyond.",
